<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Vocabulary Sizes</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides tables of vocabulary sizes for <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=40</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table VII ‣ Appendix B Vocabulary Sizes ‣ Tokenizing Semantic Segmentation with RLE" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a>), <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=80</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table VIII ‣ Appendix B Vocabulary Sizes ‣ Tokenizing Semantic Segmentation with RLE" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a>), and <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=160" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>160</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=160</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#A2.T10" title="Table X ‣ Appendix B Vocabulary Sizes ‣ Tokenizing Semantic Segmentation with RLE" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">X</span></a>) for video segmentation with varying <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and all the encoding strategies proposed in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="IV Video Segmentation ‣ Tokenizing Semantic Segmentation with RLE" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>.
These tables demonstrate the exponential increase in the number of TAC tokens with <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can also see that, for smaller mask sizes (e.g. <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=40</annotation></semantics></math>), straightforward 3D mask flattening remains more practicable for higher values of <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> due to its linear increase in <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that these tables only account for limits on <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and not those on <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, which would likely limit <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to lower values depending on the complexity of the dataset.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE VII</span>: </span><span id="A2.T7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Vocabulary sizes (<math id="A2.T7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>)
for video segmentation with <math id="A2.T7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=40</annotation></semantics></math>, varying values of <math id="A2.T7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A2.T7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.T7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=20</annotation></semantics></math> and both binary and multiclass cases.
The maximum possible <math id="A2.T7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> for each case such that <math id="A2.T7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V&lt;32K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V&lt;32K</annotation></semantics></math> is highligted in yellow.
<math id="A2.T7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is shown in units of thousands.
</span></figcaption>
